     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol are generally given in combination as initial treatment of active TB"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Isoniazid (INH): usually 5 mg/kg (max 300 mg) PO once daily; good tissue and CSF penetration; highly bactericidal"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "INH adverse effects: rash, fever, hepatotoxicity, peripheral neuropathy; rarely anemia and agranulocytosis. Safe in pregnancy"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "INH neuropathy comes from pyridoxine (B6) deficiency; pyridoxine 25–50 mg PO daily can prevent it in at-risk patients (pregnant or breastfeeding, undernutrition, diabetes, HIV, alcohol use disorder, cancer, uremia, older adults)"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "INH delays phenytoin metabolism (lower the phenytoin dose)"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Pyrazinamide (PZA): 30–40 mg/kg (max 2 g) PO once daily; bactericidal. Adverse effects: GI upset, hepatitis, and often mild hyperuricemia (rarely gout). Can be stopped after the 2-month intensive phase"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Ethambutol (EMB): 15–25 mg/kg (max 2.5 g) PO once daily; best tolerated of the first-line drugs. Main toxicity optic neuritis (first sign: can't tell blue from green); baseline color vision and acuity, then monthly questions about vision"
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
